  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Thursday...\n\nLatest surface analysis this afternoon depicts strong surface high \npressure still centered over southeastern Canada with east-\nnortheasterly flow across central Indiana. Meanwhile a low aloft \ncontinues to meander near the region. Satellite imagery shows \nextensive low clouds across the region associated with broad \ncyclonic flow from the low and lingering low-level moisture. \n\nThe latest IND ACARS sounding depicts a subsidence inversion around \n850 mb which is trapping this moisture. The stratus deck will likely \npersist through the overnight into at least Thursday morning due to \nthe inversion. Isolated light showers and drizzle will also be \npossible this evening into tonight as ceilings lower. Weak overall \nforcing will keep QPF amounts very light. Clouds have also limited \ndiurnal temperature swings. Temperatures in the 60s this afternoon \nare only expected to fall into the mid-upper 50s overnight. \n\nSome gradual clearing is expected through the day Thursday as the \naforementioned high builds further into the region, but mostly \ncloudy conditions are still expected. Drier air will filter in \nThursday night into Friday as the high pressure system continues to \nbuild in. This should help clear out the low clouds considerably. \nMax temperatures on Thursday were lowered a few degrees as low \nclouds are expected to persist. Cooler-drier air advecting into the \nregion Thursday night should cool temperatures into the upper 40s to \nto mid 50s.\n\nFriday Through Wednesday...\n\nIncreasing subsidence from upper ridging shifting eastward and \nsurface high pressure will keep weather conditions quiet. Less cloud \ncover compared to today should also favor a slight moderation in \ntemperatures, closer to normal for this time of the year. Min RH \nvalues dropping below 40 percent across south central Indiana which \ncombined with the drier conditions of late, may need to be monitored \nfor a low-end fire danger. Near calm winds will help to limit that \nthreat though.\n\nLooking further into early next week, temperatures in the lower 80s \nlook to return due to increasing warm air advection. Gradually \nincreasing rain chances can also be expected late Tuesday into \nWednesday ahead of an approaching system. Long range guidance also \ndepicts anomalous moisture surging northward ahead of this system \nwhich could support soaking rains. However, exact details remain \nuncertain due to diverging model solutions.",
